3 PAGE The Courier-Express - DuBois, PA, Wednesday, April 13, 2011   http://www.archives.com/member/Default.aspx?_act=ObituaryDetailPage&LastName=Askey&Location=PA&UniqueId=33112670
3 DATA
4 TEXT John Dayton Askey    ASKEY John Dayton Askey, 89, Frenchville, died Tuesday at Mt. Laurel Nursing Home, Clearfield. Born March 14, 1922, in Croft, he was a son of the late Roy Ralph Askey and Mary Ellen Waring Askey. He was married to the former G
5 CONC race Louise Herr Dec. 30, 1940, in Clearfield. She survives. A lifelong resident of the Karthaus, Keewaydin, Frenchville area, he was a veteran of World War II. He served with G Company, 329th Infantry, 83rd Infantry Division where he was wounde
5 CONC d at Liege, Belgium, during the Battle of the Bulge, and the 313th Troop Carrier. His numerous awards include the Combat Infantry Badge, Purple Heart and Bronze Star. He had been an independent coal mine operator for several years until his healt
5 CONC h forced him to quit the mines and retrain as a welder and maintenance mechanic. In 1969, at the age of 50, he completed high school. He retired from Piper Aircraft Corp. at Quehanna in 1983 after 17 years of service. He was a deputy wildlife cons
5 CONC ervation officer for 13 years. He served on the Covington Township School Board. He was member of the Karthaus Volunteer Fire Company, Oak Hill Grange 717, the 83rd Infantry Division, Battle of the Bulge Association, Mosquito Creek Sportsmen&apos;
5 CONC s Club and Frenchville Veterans of Foreign Wars. He was a member of the Church of Christ at Snow Shoe where he was a song leader for over 40 years. He is survived by one daughter, Linda Lou Askey (Thomas) Kronenwetter, Weedville; one son, Ronald J
5 CONC ohn Askey, Lumber Bridge, N. C.; three grandchildren; three great-granddaughters; and several nieces and nephews. He was preceded in death by one sister, Mary Elizabeth Askey Clark and two brothers, Millard Leonard Askey and William Ralph Askey
5 CONC . q q q ASKEY: John Dayton, Frenchville, died April 12, 2011. Friends will be received from 6-8 p.m. Friday and 9-10 a.m. Saturday at Strange &amp; Weaver Funeral Home, Morrisdale. A funeral service will be held at 10 a.m. Saturday at the funera
5 CONC l home with Minister Keith Newell officiating. Military honors will be accorded by Leige-Renaud Veterans of Foreign Wars Post No. 8386, Frenchville. Burial will be at Keewaydin Cemetery, Frenchville. Memorial donations may be made to Karthaus Ambu
5 CONC lance Service, P. O. Box 9, Karthaus, PA 16843 or Church of Christ Building Fund, Snow Shoe, PA 16874.
