1 CONC Birth:  Jul. 12, 1839
1 CONT Republic
1 CONT Seneca County
1 CONT Ohio, USA
1 CONT Death:  Jan. 16, 1935
1 CONT Kalamazoo
1 CONT Kalamazoo County
1 CONT Michigan, USA
1 CONT
1 CONT Jennie E. Askey was married to Joseph L. Morlan on Dec. 4, 1862 in LaGrange Co., IN.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joseph L. Morlan was born in 1834 in Columbiana Co., Ohio. Joseph is the son of Joseph Morlan, Jr. and Myalmay Jane "Alma" Hayes.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to an obiturary for Joseph L. Morlan, published 29 Aug 1878 in the Waterloo Press, it said that Joseph grew to manhood in Columbiana county, Ohio, teaching school and studying law. About 1859 he moved to LaGrange, LaGrange Co., IN, wher
1 CONC e he was a deputy county clerk. During the rebellion (Civil War), he was a clerk in the office of the state Supreme Court and in the Governor's office, in Indianapolis. After leaving Indianapolis, he resided for a short time in Ft. Wayne and in La
1 CONC Grange, then moved to Waterloo, DeKalb county, Indiana. There, he became Prosecuting Attorney for two terms.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ref: Ancestry.com Joseph L. Morlan Obit.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1879, Jennie moved, with her five children, to Kalamazoo, Kalamazoo County, Michigan.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jennie was a daughter and only child of Thomas Askey (b. PA) and his wife Betsy Robinson (b. Mass.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Jennie Morlan, 95, Ohio Native, Dies
1 CONT
1 CONT Funeral of Mrs. H. E. Ralston's Mother Will be Held Tuesday
1 CONT
1 CONT Death claimed another of Kalamazoo's pioneers Tuesday night when Mrs. Jennie E, Morlan, 95, died at the home of her daughter Mrs. H. E. Ralston, 717 West South Street, where she had lived for the last 20 years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mrs. Morlan was born July 12, 1839, in Republic, [Seneca Co.] Ohio. In 1861 [Dec. 4, 1862 in LaGrange Co.] she married Joseph L. Morlan, attorney, and they moved to Waterloo, Indiana, where Mr. Morlan died in 1878. The next year Mrs. Morlan, wit
1 CONC h her five children, moved to Kalamazoo, where she since has made her home. Four of her five children preceded Mrs. Morlan in death.
1 CONT
1 CONT ACCOMPLISHED MUSICIAN
1 CONT
1 CONT For a number of years the deceased was a member of the Seventh Day Adventist church where she was active in church affairs. She was an accomplished musician, her piano improvisations being a pleasure to all. She was adept at sewing, and until a fe
1 CONC w weeks ago did all her own sewing. She was widely read, her eyesight remaining unimpaired to the end.
1 CONT
1 CONT FUNERAL THURSDAY
1 CONT
1 CONT Surviving, besides the daughter, Mrs. Ralston, are one son-in-law, U. G. Grandbois; nine grandchildren: Mrs. Harvey M. Stewart, Mrs. Merrill B. King, Mrs. Howard C. Bush, Cameron L. Davis, Morlan J. Grandbois and Ralph M. Ralston of Kalamazoo, Gor
1 CONC don W. Davis and Mrs. Robert F. Baxter of Detroit, and Lowell A. Morlan of Denver. Thirteen great grandchildren also survive
1 CONT
1 CONT Funeral services in charge of Rev. Thomas Laity, will be held at 4 Thursday afternoon at the Truesdale chapel, with burial in Riverside. Pallbearers will be six grandsons.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ref: Kalamazoo Gazette, Jan. 18, 1935
1 CONT
