1 CONT Cyrus G. Clouser or dies at Harrisburg Hospital.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cyrus Grafton Clouser, of Harrisburg, a former resident of this place of this place, (New Bloomfield), died at the polyclinic hospital Friday afternoon last following an operation for appendicitis. Some two weeks before he was seized with acute pa
1 CONC in in the region of his appendix, but relief was afforded. A week later while walking in the evening in the city he was again seized and was assisted home. Some days later he was taken to the hospital for treatment. When the operation was performe
1 CONC d it was discovered that the appendix was badly diseased and that that gangrene had already set in.
1 CONT
1 CONT The deceased was the son of the late Willis K. and Jenny Elizabeth (Askins) clouds are and was bored in Centre Township September 9, 1897, and was aged 23 years, five months and nine days.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is survived by Annie G. and Louise, Mrs. Charles Berkholder, of Harrisburg and Charles W. Clouser, of Vandergrift, Pa.. After the death of his father the family moved to Harrisburg. He had listed in the service in the world war and served in th
1 CONC e quartermasters department and was overseas for six months, and was mustered out at Camp Dix, New Jersey at the time of his death he was engaged in the supply department of Elliott Fisher Co.,
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a young man of fine character industrious and thrifty. He was a member of Trinity Reform Church of this place joined in his early teens and was also a member of the Knights of Malta, patriotic order Sons of America, American Legion and th
1 CONC e Veterans of Foreign Wars.
1 CONT
1 CONT The remains of the deceased were brought here Saturday to the home of his uncle Mr. Cyrus Clouser are and wife. Funeral services were held there Monday at 2 PM, his pastor Rev. J. Tomas Fox, of this place, officiating. Preaching from the text Jame
1 CONC s 4:14-- quote whereas the know not what shall be on tomorrow for what is your life it is even a vapor that appear at for a little time and then vanish away." His remains were born to their last resting place in the cemetery by six of his comrade
1 CONC s all World War I veterans; B.F. Keller, David Miller, Paul G. Swartz, Edgar M. Garber, John W. Clouser, and Joe G. Darlington.
1 CONT
1 CONT Published Perry County Times
1 CONT February, 24, 1921
