1 CONC Birth: 	Oct. 23, 1888
1 CONT Ord
1 CONT Valley County
1 CONT Nebraska, USA
1 CONT Death: 	Jun. 2, 1956
1 CONT Butte County
1 CONT California, USA
1 CONT
1 CONT TALMAGE E. LOSEE - A stalwart and wide-awake young horticulturist, who is making a specialty of almonds, is Talmage E. Losee, popularly known as "Tal," who was born at Ord, Valley County, Nebr., on October 23, 1888, and came to California in 190
1 CONC 0 with his father's family, settling near Chico, Butte County. Here his father, Herbert Losee, has been more than ordinarily successful as a farmer, becoming, in fact, one of the leading almond growers of the state; so that he is today regarded a
1 CONC s an authority on almond culture. He conducts a nursery in connection with his horticultural establishment; and while yet a mere boy, Talmage learned there how to graft, prune, plant and care for almond trees.
1 CONT
1 CONT Talmage Losee now owns forty acres of excellent river-bottom land near East Biggs, which he bought in 1911, after having widely searched for a place where conditions were just right for almond culture. He has a fine young almond orchard of ten acr
1 CONC es, and has wisely planted the remaining thirty acres to alfalfa. He has also dug a large well, which furnishes a never-failing supply of water both for his stock and for irrigation purposes. He has installed a pumping plant with a six-inch centri
1 CONC fugal pump, run by a fifteen-horse-power electric motor, and with a twenty-foot lift. He has constructed irrigation ditches, and uses galvanized iron pipes even for irrigating his alfalfa. He also has a nursery where he propagates choice fruit tre
1 CONC es; and since he takes great care in furnishing only trees of the best strains and the greatest vitality, his stock and enterprise have met with great favor. In addition, Mr. Losee has a number of high-grade Holstein cattle (milch cows and heifers
1 CONC ) and raises some choice hogs. With such a beginning, it is only a question of a few years before young Mr. Losee's fame as an agriculturist will be widely known, thus perpetuating the celebrity of his highly-honored father. Source: "History of Bu
1 CONC tte County, Cal.," by George C. Mansfield, Page 1297, Historic Record Co, Los Angeles, CA, 1918.
1 CONT
